# Run output and run-to-run state are artifacts, never sources.
#
# ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH, same reasoning as /pnpm-lock.yaml below: an
# unanchored `state/` matches at ANY depth and silently swallowed this
# ticket's own tests/fixtures/state/ fixtures (STATE-01, lib/state.sh) until
# it was anchored - already-tracked fixtures would have survived, so the
# damage would have stayed invisible until the next state/ fixture was
# silently never added.
/reports/
/state/

# Suite-run artifacts, never sources.  tools/daily-suite.sh writes its own
# results under $SCOURSH_DAILY_DIR (outside the checkout), but a leg run by hand
# can still drop these here.
suite.log
findings-*/
findings.normalised.jsonl

# macOS Finder metadata.  An untracked .DS_Store was enough to make fleet
# tooling refuse this clone as dirty.
.DS_Store

# Generated local DAST test-target state: the two throwaway identities'
# secret-files and the auth.conf referencing them (tools/dast-test-identities.sh).
# Machine-specific and regenerable; never sources, never committed.
.dast-test-target/

# The two vendored databases are GENERATED, on a networked box, by
# `tools/vendor-engines.sh advisories` - never during a scan, and never in this
# repository (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 25).  Both are absent by default in
# every checkout, which is what lets a consumer tell "nobody has run the
# vendoring pipeline" apart from "it ran and found nothing" - two different
# facts that a committed database collapses into one.
#
# data/versions.db was committed by accident once (commit 9b580c1), carrying
# four fixture rows from somebody's local verification run - `goodpkg`,
# `uspkg`, `critpkg`, `lowpkg`, with `# bulk:` provenance headers naming
# /tmp scratch paths.  That shipped fake vulnerability data in the release
# artifact and made a fresh clone read as a populated database.  These two
# lines are why it cannot happen silently again; commit either file only by
# deliberate `git add -f`, and only with real, pinned provenance.
data/advisories.db
data/versions.db

# `pnpm test` / `npm test` drop these even though package.json declares no
# dependency of any kind (see AGENTS.md: it exists ONLY so the conventional
# entry point runs tests/run-tests.sh).  scoursh has no Node runtime dependency
# and neither file is a source; an untracked one is enough to make fleet tooling
# refuse this clone as dirty, exactly as .DS_Store above was.
#
# ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH, and that is load-bearing rather than tidiness:
# an unanchored `pnpm-lock.yaml` / `package-lock.json` matches at ANY depth, and
# tests/fixtures/sca/ is full of lockfiles by those exact names.  Already-tracked
# fixtures would survive, so the damage would be invisible until the next SCA
# ticket's new fixture was silently never added.
/node_modules/
/pnpm-lock.yaml
/package-lock.json
